; regression test for a real bug: modal.kurt's axioms used to be written with plain symbols
; (`p`, `q`, `A`, `B`) instead of `%`-prefixed schema variables (unlike every other theory in
; this repo), which silently pinned every axiom to those exact two hardcoded propositions --
; `K` could only ever be applied to the literal `p`/`q` declared in the file, never to any other
; boolean expression. Confirmed the bug directly before fixing it: `use □(X ⇒ Y)` + `use □X`
; could never derive `□X ⇒ □Y`, only the literal `□(p ⇒ q)` + `□p` could. Fixed by rewriting the
; axioms with `%p`/`%q`. This proof deliberately uses `X`/`Y`, not `p`/`q`, specifically so it
; would have caught the bug (confirmed: reverting the fix breaks exactly this file).
;
; note: kurt's derivation is single-hop, so applying `K` (a chained implication, not a
; conjunction-antecedent) needs the intermediate `□X ⇒ □Y` written out explicitly before the
; final `□Y` -- see doc/kurt-doc.md's "known gaps" (single-hop derivation).
load modal
bool X, Y

use □(X ⇒ Y)
use □X

□X ⇒ □Y
□Y
